# An `@` argument as the call hands it over, and the one thing a call still
# owes after it (design.md §4.8 copy in, copy out; §4.10 copy-on-write;
# panel 196's R1; M-robustness-guards step 1).
#
# **An `@` argument is its place, never a copy, since panel 196's R1**
# (ratified 2026-10-06; defect 413). The emitter hands the callee the place's
# own address: `&h0_b.f_items` for `@b.items`, `&(*ph0_s)` for an `@`
# parameter passed on (`emit/places.hero`'s `slot_lvalue`), and for an
# element, `@bs[0]`, the array unshared first and then the element's own
# slot in the runtime's block (`places.reached`). Inside Heroes that is
# still copy in, copy out, because an `@` place is exclusive: two `@`
# arguments of one place are refused (`resolve/writes.hero`, defect 011), and
# a by-value argument whose header the call writes through `@` is held for
# the call (`ir/held.hero`), so nothing the callee reads can see a write it
# makes before it returns.
#
# **What this module did until R1**, and why it stopped. An element of a
# copy-on-write array has no C lvalue the emitter could spell, so the element
# was read into a temporary before the call, the callee wrote the temporary,
# and the temporary was stored back through the indexed place after it: Ada's
# copy-back, Swift's materialised `inout` on a subscript. Two rules kept that
# honest (the index evaluated once, the write-back owed by the call), and it
# was right for every reader inside Heroes. It was wrong for C, which was
# handed the temporary: zlib knows its stream by address and refused every
# call after the first (`0 -2 -2`), and libuv kept two addresses of the
# caller's frame in its loop. Until 2026-09-03 the emitter had handed the
# callee `&h0_bs`, the whole array, and died at exit 138; the element's own
# address is what both repairs were reaching for.
#
# **The index is still evaluated once and left to right**: `lower.place`
# lowered every index expression on its walk (§4.8), and the place carries
# those values to the emitter, which unshares and descends at the call, after
# every argument was computed. So `xs[f()]` calls `f` once, and a place that
# a nested argument's own `@` call changed is reached as it stands when the
# call is made, as the copy-in of a whole cell always was.
#
# **What remains here is the marked cell**: a parameter declared
# `owned <freer>` is handed a hidden `cstr` cell C only writes, and the call
# owes its release after it (`ir/owned_release.hero`), which `Args.after`
# carries.

use ast
use token
use check/table
use check/state
use check/freer as freer
use ir
use ir/containers
use ir/build

# What one call's arguments became, and what the call still owes.
record Args
    run: ir.Run
    after: [WriteBack]

# One marked cell to release into its place once the call returns: a
# parameter carrying `owned <freer>`, whose bytes are copied out, the
# original freed, and the `str?` stored through the author's place. Paid by
# `ir/owned_release.hero`, which is where the freer and the library's
# `validated` are reachable; this module cannot do it, because
# `owned_release` uses it and Heroes refuses module cycles. `at` is the
# parameter's position, which the mark is read at. Until panel 196's R1 an
# element's temporary was a write-back too, stored by this module; an
# element is its own place since, so a cell is the only thing a call owes.
record WriteBack
    place: ir.Place
    slot: i64
    at: i64

# The inout argument for `spot`: the place itself, whatever its path (an
# element's included, the module doc above says why), or a HIDDEN `cstr` cell,
# recorded in `after`, when the parameter carries `owned <freer>`.
#
# `callee` is the declaration this argument is going to, absent for a record
# construction, a built-in and an indirect call — none of which can carry a
# mark, because a mark is written on an `extern`. `at` is this argument's
# parameter position, which the mark is read at.
function argument(@b: build.Lowering, c: state.Checked, decls: [ast.Decl], span: token.Span, spot: ir.Place, callee: i64?, at: i64, @after: [WriteBack]) -> ir.Arg
    # **The marked cell, whatever the place is.** §4.19 says the cell is only
    # WRITTEN, so nothing is read out of the caller's place: C is handed a
    # cell of its own, and what lands in the author's place afterwards is the
    # copy.
    if !callee.is_err() && freer.owned_parameter(decls, at: callee.must(), index: at)
        cell = build.synthetic(@b, prefix: "cell", ty: table.cstr_ty(c.types))

        # **Initialised to null, and that is robustness rather than tidiness**
        # (§12; CLAUDE.md §7 makes a refcounted slot the only other exception
        # to the no-initialisation rule). A C function that takes an error
        # cell and returns success may never write it — SQLite does write a
        # null, other libraries do not promise to — and a release over an
        # uninitialised cell would free a stack pattern. With the store, an
        # untouched cell takes the release's own NULL branch and the program
        # sees `fail("null_cstr", …)`, which is the truth.
        #
        # **Nothing that RUNS on this Mac can see this store missing**, and
        # that was measured rather than assumed (2026-09-07): with it deleted,
        # the case written for the shape prints the same four lines at `-O0`
        # and under `-fsanitize=address,undefined`, because the C local is
        # indeterminate and happened to be zero. What guards it is
        # `tests/golden/ir/owned-cell.hero`, where `UPDATE_GOLDEN=1` is
        # forbidden. Panel 116 reached the same conclusion about the NULL
        # guard three instructions below this one.
        empty = build.emit(@b, op: .lit(value: .null_ptr), ty: table.cstr_ty(c.types), span: span)
        build.store_slot(@b, slot_id: cell, value: empty, span: span)
        after @ after.push(WriteBack(place: spot, slot: cell, at: at))
        return .inout_arg(place: build.whole(cell))
    return .inout_arg(place: spot)

# Whether an `@` argument of these runs through an index. Only a CALL may
# hand one over, because the emitter writes the array's unshares at a call
# and nowhere else (`emit/inst.hero`); a record construction or an abort
# that carried one would hand C an element of a block another value still
# shares. `ir/verify.hero` asks it of every construction and abort.
function through_index(f: containers.Function, run: ir.Run) -> bool
    for arg in containers.args_of(f, run)
        match arg
            .value_arg => continue
            .inout_arg io =>
                for step in containers.steps_of(f, io.place.path)
                    match step
                        .index_step => return true
                        .field_step => continue

    return false
